# REQUIRES: llvm-as, llvm-dis, opt
#
# MT-CM4 WITNESS: LOCAL PER-TEMPLATE CHECKS DO NOT DETERMINE GLOBAL TRACE ORDER.
#
# Metatheory section 0.2 lists MT-CM4's invalid principle as "local per-template
# checks determine global trace order"; section 15 states the invalid rule as
# "equal payload, occurrence, and count for each static event template imply
# equal whole traces" and exhibits
#
#   rho_1 = [event_a(0), event_b(0)]
#   rho_2 = [event_b(0), event_a(0)]
#
# The normative side this defends: the specification's section 7 says the
# projected trace is "the concatenation, in execution order, of every Project_A
# word", so "presence, multiplicity, global order, failure, and termination are
# therefore part of one trace rather than separate optional checks", and
# SiteOrderAlignment (spec section on alignment coordinate families) compares
# aligned equal-tag pairs on unequal `Site`, `DynamicVisit`, and
# `WithinStepOrdinal` -- an ordering coordinate, not a count.
#
# integration/Inputs/metatheory-cm4/order_swap.ll is that pair, selected by a
# secret bit: both arms write the SAME payload to the SAME two public sinks in
# OPPOSITE order.
#
# WHAT THIS TEST CHECKS: that the fixture really does have equal per-template
# multiplicity and payload across the two arms while the aligned order differs,
# and that the ordered assertions are the only ones a repair kills.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT CHECK: nothing here executes relational semantics, aligns
# events, or computes a status. There is no ModelStatus, no NFConforms, no
# receipt, and no claim about the 22.1.8 normative boundary. This is a
# PreflightV1 shape pin for a per-row event checker that does not exist yet.

# RUN: %llvm-as %harness/integration/Inputs/metatheory-cm4/order_swap.ll -o %t.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.bc -o %t.ll

# --- half one: every template-local quantity is EQUAL across the two arms ----
#
# Four release-shaped stores, two per arm. Asserted as cardinalities, because
# presence is the easy half and MT-CM4 is precisely about the hard half.
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8' %t.ll > %t.total.count
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TOTAL --input-file=%t.total.count
# TOTAL: {{^4$}}

# Per static event template, count 2: one occurrence in each arm.
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8 0, ptr %%event_a' %t.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TEMPLATE-A
# TEMPLATE-A: {{^2$}}
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8 0, ptr %%event_b' %t.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TEMPLATE-B
# TEMPLATE-B: {{^2$}}

# Payload is literally the same constant everywhere, so a payload comparison
# has nothing to report either.
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8 0,' %t.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PAYLOAD
# PAYLOAD: {{^4$}}

# --- half two: the GLOBAL order differs, and it is the secret that picks it --
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ORDER --input-file=%t.ll

# ORDER-LABEL: define void @mt_cm4_order_selected(i8 %secret, ptr %event_a, ptr %event_b)
# ORDER: %bit = and i8 %secret, 1
# ORDER-NEXT: %high = icmp ne i8 %bit, 0
# ORDER-NEXT: br i1 %high, label %order_ab, label %order_ba
# ORDER-LABEL: order_ab:
# ORDER-NEXT: store i8 0, ptr %event_a
# ORDER-NEXT: store i8 0, ptr %event_b
# ORDER-LABEL: order_ba:
# ORDER-NEXT: store i8 0, ptr %event_b
# ORDER-NEXT: store i8 0, ptr %event_a

# --- negative arm 1: an order repair is INVISIBLE to the local checks --------
#
# Rewrite the second arm to use the same order as the first. This is the
# repaired program: the order leak is gone. Every count above is unchanged,
# which is the whole content of MT-CM4.
# RUN: %python -c "import pathlib,sys; t=pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]).read_text(); i=t.index('order_ba:'); j=t.index('br label', i); b=t[i:j].replace('%%event_b','@@SWAP@@').replace('%%event_a','%%event_b').replace('@@SWAP@@','%%event_a'); pathlib.Path(sys.argv[2]).write_text(t[:i]+b+t[j:])" %t.ll %t.aligned.ll

# RUN: grep -c 'store i8' %t.aligned.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TOTAL
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8 0, ptr %%event_a' %t.aligned.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TEMPLATE-A
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8 0, ptr %%event_b' %t.aligned.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TEMPLATE-B
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8 0,' %t.aligned.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PAYLOAD

# The ordered assertions, and only the ordered assertions, die.
# RUN: not %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=ORDER --input-file=%t.aligned.ll 2>&1 | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=KILLED
# KILLED: error: ORDER-NEXT{{:}} is not on the line after the previous match
# KILLED-NEXT: ORDER-NEXT{{:}} store i8 0, ptr %event_b
# KILLED: 'next' match was here
# KILLED-NEXT: store i8 0, ptr %event_b

# --- negative arm 2: the local checks are not vacuous either -----------------
#
# Drop the event_b occurrence from the first arm only. Now per-template
# multiplicity really does differ between the arms, and that IS a local fact,
# so the count assertions must move.
# RUN: %python -c "import pathlib,sys; t=pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]).read_text(); i=t.index('order_ab:'); j=t.index('br label', i); b=''.join(l for l in t[i:j].splitlines(True) if 'ptr %%event_b' not in l); pathlib.Path(sys.argv[2]).write_text(t[:i]+b+t[j:])" %t.ll %t.dropped.ll

# RUN: grep -c 'store i8' %t.dropped.ll > %t.dropped.count
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=DROPPED --input-file=%t.dropped.count
# DROPPED: {{^3$}}
# RUN: not %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=TOTAL --input-file=%t.dropped.count 2>&1 | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=COUNT-KILLED
# COUNT-KILLED: error: TOTAL{{:}} expected string not found in input

# --- the leak is not an artifact of the unoptimised shape --------------------
#
# At -O2 the secret branch disappears, both arms fuse, and the static store
# count halves from 4 to 2 -- so a static per-template counter now sees ONE
# occurrence of each template and even less than before. The order dependence
# survives as two secret-selected store addresses. This is why the ordering
# coordinate has to live in the aligned trace comparison and not in a
# per-template tally.
# RUN: %opt -O2 -S %t.ll -o %t.o2.ll
# RUN: grep -c 'store i8' %t.o2.ll | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OPT-TOTAL
# OPT-TOTAL: {{^2$}}
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OPT --input-file=%t.o2.ll
# OPT: = and i8 %secret, 1
# OPT: = select i1 {{.*}}, ptr %event_b, ptr %event_a
# OPT-NEXT: = select i1 {{.*}}, ptr %event_a, ptr %event_b
# OPT-NEXT: store i8 0, ptr
# OPT-NEXT: store i8 0, ptr
